  "hypothesis": "Combining flattened contiguous storage (A), a deterministic max-heap top-k selection keyed by (distance, original index) (A/B), NEON-vectorized distance computation with scalar tail (B), and direct frequency counting with signed-character tie-breaking (C) yields an exact KNN implementation that avoids OpenMP thread overhead while preserving reference semantics including sentinel handling when fewer than k points exist.",
  "notes": "Kept the original header unchanged. The knn() function flattens groups into xs/ys/labels arrays, computes squared distances using NEON intrinsics on ARM64 (scalar fallback otherwise), selects the exact k nearest neighbors via a max-heap with index tie-breaking, and counts label frequencies with sentinel labels added when total points < k. Tie-breaking for equal maximum frequency follows signed char ordering to match qsort-based reference behavior.",
